# ── 03A · INFERENCE PROVIDERS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Ordinary authenticated GPT remains the default. `sandcodex local MODEL` instead uses a pinned Ollama runtime, or an
# explicit `--local-ollama-bin PATH` host/prebuilt binary, acquires the requested model when necessary, verifies its
# source digest, derives any selected runner parameters into another digest-checked model profile, and checks context,
# loaded placement, Responses-instructions delivery, streaming, and strict tool calls before giving the real Codex
# frontend access through a narrow loopback relay.
# Qualification is a sampled interface check, not an intelligence benchmark or proof of prompt-injection resistance.
# Model weights and the host-side inference runtime are complex untrusted inputs; content identity is evidence, not
# provenance or safety. Local inference does not grant root, trust project configuration, or loosen tool policy.
# The server posture is launcher policy rather than ambient tuning: one loaded model, one parallel request, an
# eight-request queue, Flash Attention, f16 KV cache, and five-minute idle retention. CPU admission adds an f16 KV-cache
# estimate from provider-advertised model geometry and a 1 GiB runtime reserve before attempting the real bounded load.
# The receipt preserves the estimate, the managed memory envelope, and their limits; successful loading, not arithmetic,
# remains the fit observation. `--local-memory-mib` is an explicit operator override for the runtime cgroup/admission
# envelope; it may be useful on RAM-constrained CUDA experiments where the GPU carries only a slice of the model.
# Ollama receives private Sandcodex-owned HOME, XDG, and temporary roots so managed inference does not use `~/.ollama`
# or scatter provider state through the operator's ordinary home directories.
#
# `sandcodex remote MODEL` selects one private per-user HTTPS Responses descriptor. The credential value is read from
# the descriptor's named environment variable, copied to trusted state, removed from the launch environment, and
# supplied only through Codex's command-backed frontend authentication. A bounded synthetic qualification runs before
# the first launch for each descriptor/model/probe tuple. It proves sampled protocol behavior, never immutable hosted
# model bytes, price, retention policy, future behavior, or universal compatibility. `openai_api` and `openrouter` are
# built-in descriptors; custom descriptors remain private per-user catalog rows.
# `sandcodex remote auto` adds a launch-bound loopback Responses relay between Codex and OpenRouter's exact
# `/api/v1/responses` endpoint. It buffers and validates a complete upstream response, reversibly flattens namespace,
# custom, and deferred-search tools. It preserves call IDs and may try another bound free agent-candidate/account
# route only before any headers or bytes become visible to Codex. The safe commit point deliberately sacrifices
# incremental token display. A bound resume restarts the exact relay artifact and protected bank digests. No retry
# occurs after visible output, and a live conversation never silently replaces its sealed bank.
#
# `sandcodex gemini auto` keeps that same Codex frontend and starts the adapter embedded in this artifact: Codex speaks
# Responses over a private random-bearer loopback seam, while the adapter speaks stateless Gemini Interactions v1 to
# Google AI Studio. It translates the Codex message, reasoning, structured-output, function, custom-tool, MCP,
# tool-search, multimodal-input, usage, error, and streaming shapes exercised by qualification. Opaque signed Gemini
# model steps are preserved exactly for tool continuation and resume; malformed or reordered state fails closed.
# Before any semantic output is visible, the adapter keeps the strongest free model selected while rotating through
# the five-account key bank on quota, rate, authentication, or permission pressure. It moves down the healthy model
# ladder only after every account route for the stronger model is cooling, and may wait a bounded interval when all
# routes are cooling. Signed tool continuations preserve the exact history through account rotation and model fallback.
# Companion workers inherit this exact parent-owned adapter and route without receiving the Google credentials.
#
# New local, remote, and Gemini conversations are bound to their provider evidence and exact canonical project. Resume
# them with the same explicit namespace—`sandcodex local MODEL --RESUME`, `sandcodex remote MODEL --RESUME`, or
# `sandcodex gemini auto --RESUME`. Sandcodex verifies
# the binding before provider startup and never silently substitutes GPT, another endpoint, another model, a smaller
# context, or a newly pulled mutable tag. Plain `sandcodex --RESUME [LIST]` remains Codex's native GPT continuation.
# Native Codex also remains the transcript parser: Sandcodex reads only a bounded identity prefix when it must route an
# alternate provider, and never interprets, repairs, rewrites, or moves the conversation body. Managed local mode also
# generates a one-model Codex catalog from the exact qualification receipt and uses a compact governing briefing; the
# complete room contract remains available through `sandcodex-env --capabilities`.
#
# ── 04 · AUTHORITY POSTURES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DEFAULT SAFE ROOM
#   Filesystem               selected project read/write; system and Nix store read-only; other host trees absent;
#                            trusted frontend state hidden from ordinary model tool commands
#   Process                  complete frontend tree in private user/PID/IPC/UTS/mount/net namespaces; parent-death
#                            coupling; required-service watchdog
#   Kernel                   host kernel shared; capabilities dropped; keyring syscalls denied; nested command sandbox
#                            remains active
#   Network                  direct routes absent; explicit loopback proxy bridge; default unrestricted HTTP/HTTPS/SOCKS
#                            including LAN, or provider-only when `--restricted-network` is selected
#   Resources                transient user systemd cgroup unit; 50% host memory, no swap, 2048 tasks. A scope is
#                            preferred; a service with a private one-use environment handoff covers callers in sibling
#                            session cgroups. Safe failure remains mandatory unless `--no-resource-scope` is explicit;
#                            that degradation also disables the cgroup-backed inner jail for the launch.
#   Project trust            `.codex`, hooks, MCP declarations, IDE files, and shell startup files are mutable project
#                            content but are not loaded as frontend policy unless `--trust-project-config` is chosen
#
# BARE METAL
#   `--BARE-METAL` removes Sandcodex's outer containment, preserves the invoking environment/PATH, and, by default,
#   runs `sudo -v`. Sandcodex never reads the password; sudo reads the terminal and Sandcodex refreshes only that ticket
#   while Codex lives. Before provider launch it proves `sudo -n -- id -u` returns 0, and Codex is instructed to use
#   `sudo -n` for root work so no model-controlled command can stall on a password prompt. Add `--no-sudo-ticket` when
#   the host-uncontained room should not touch sudo at launch; root commands may still work later if the host already
#   has an ambient reusable ticket. This is intentional host administration authority. The inner jail remains available
#   as a voluntary delegation tool, not a parent boundary.
#
# INNER JAIL
#   `sandcodex-jail` lets the primary agent hand a smaller, fresh boundary to a less-trusted action. The action controls
#   argv only. The delegator controls tool closure, workspace/cache, network, time, CPU, memory, swap, PIDs,
#   file/tmp/home ceilings, sealed inputs, and declared result evidence. Grants recurse only by narrowing. The jail is
#   for suspect commands and reproducible evidence; it is not where the primary agent must perform ordinary work.
#
# OPERATOR SERVICE
#   `--operator-service FILE` freezes one reviewed declaration at launch and runs that application in a credentialless
#   sibling boundary. An authenticated host-loopback ingress and one-time bootstrap support a durable cockpit. It is the
#   preferred durable-service mechanism when argv and authority are known before launch. Socket-aware applications may
#   consume the inherited listener and readiness descriptors directly. Ordinary servers may instead declare a
#   `tcp-loopback` adapter: Sandcodex waits for their exact private target port, emits readiness on their behalf, and
#   bridges the stable listener without granting frontend credentials or a network route. Runtime controls use
#   generation numbers to prevent stale stop/restart requests.
