DEFAULT BOUNDARY
  The complete Codex process tree runs under an outer bubblewrap boundary with
  a synthetic root plus private mount, PID, IPC, UTS, user, and network
  namespaces. Keyring syscalls are denied. Direct sockets have no route; a
  host-side proxy is reachable through a private Unix-socket bridge. It permits
  unrestricted HTTP, HTTPS CONNECT, and SOCKS egress, including LAN/loopback,
  by default. Model-generated commands retain Codex's credential-isolating
  project sandbox with full read/write/delete/execute authority over ordinary
  selected-project content, including Git metadata, hooks, agent/MCP settings,
  IDE files, and shell startup files.

  Model-tool writes: ordinary PROJECT content and authorized disposable worker state.
  Frontend writes: one hashed global control tree for auth copies and sessions.
  Hidden: every other host data tree, host runtime sockets, the global auth
  source, and saved sessions. Read-only
  runtime: /etc, /usr, /sys, /nix/store,
  and ordinary system libraries/tools. Safe runs use a transient systemd cgroup unit
  capped at 50% host memory, 2048 tasks, and no swap. A trusted per-project
  operation lock rejects every overlapping run, doctor, or cleanup, including
  overlap between safe and bare-metal postures.

  Before launch, Sandcodex reclaims inactive user-owned, single-link FIFO and
  Unix-socket pathnames left in ordinary project content. It preserves and
  rejects active or unsafe special nodes, nested mounts, external hardlinks,
  and aliases that bypass protected paths. Recursive
  Sandcodex launches fail before Nix, state setup, sudo, or provider execution;
  only the protected loop request protocol is accepted from an active sandbox.

