CODEX PASSTHROUGH
  Provider arguments follow --. In safe mode Sandcodex rejects arguments that
  replace its sandbox, approvals, project root, configuration, feature gates,
  execution rules, or provider authority. Bare metal deliberately permits them.

OPERATOR COCKPIT
  A command room may explicitly launch one project service for its complete
  lifetime. Sandcodex freezes the declaration and authority, runs the service
  in a separate credentialless bubblewrap boundary, retains its private Unix
  listener across bounded restarts, and exposes it to the ordinary desktop
  browser through an authenticated IPv4-loopback-only HTTP/WebSocket ingress:
    sandcodex --operator-service .sandcodex-operator-service.json --open-operator

  Socket-aware services consume SANDCODEX_LISTEN_FD and SANDCODEX_READY_FD.
  An optional {"mode":"tcp-loopback","target_port":3000} adapter supports
  ordinary foreground servers: it supplies target-address/port environment,
  owns readiness and bridging, and keeps the application credentialless.

  A second host terminal can inspect or control only that exact active run:
    sandcodex operator status --project DIR
    sandcodex operator open --project DIR
    sandcodex operator rotate-session --project DIR
    sandcodex operator revoke-sessions --project DIR
    sandcodex operator restart-service --project DIR --expected-generation N
    sandcodex operator stop-service --project DIR --expected-generation N

  Browser sessions are random, host-only, HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict, and
  invalid after command-room exit. Bootstrap secrets exist only in one-time
  mode-0600 HTML files. No operator declaration means no service, listener,
  loopback port, bootstrap, helper, or environment capability.

PUBLISHED TCP INGRESS
  --publish creates one supervised raw TCP path from an exact host listener to
  an agent 127.0.0.1 target. Sandcodex adds no HTTP, TLS, or authentication.
  Loopback plus SSH forwarding is the private remote-access default; a
  non-loopback bind is LAN-facing when host addressing and firewall permit it.

  Inspect another active terminal without taking its project lock, starting
  Nix, creating state, or writing a launcher log:
    sandcodex publish status --project DIR
    sandcodex publish doctor --project DIR

  Doctor adds a same-host TCP connect. Both commands distinguish launcher,
  publisher, inner relay, application readiness, and external reachability;
  only a real protocol request from the intended client proves the last two.

LOOPER
  Start three noninteractive turns with the default continuation prompt:
    sandcodex loop
  Choose a finite turn count, or use 0 until stopped or a turn fails:
    sandcodex loop --turns 20 --effort high --prompt 'Keep improving it.'
  Resume an existing session instead of opening a fresh one:
    sandcodex loop --RESUME --turns 3
  Stream Codex JSONL events, including per-turn token usage:
    sandcodex loop --turns 3 --json
  Keep a safe loop under the user systemd manager and recover quota failures:
    sandcodex loop --detach --turns 200 --auto-resume

  Detached supervisors preserve the exact subagent policy chosen at launch.
  Native Codex subagents inherit the parent sandbox and approval posture. Use
  --agent-depth 5 deliberately for recursive delegation; the thread cap still
  bounds concurrent fan-out, but deeper trees consume more tokens and disk.

  Safe loops retry quota, overload, and transient transport failures by default.
  They stop before a turn if free space falls below 2 GiB, project growth exceeds
  20 GiB, downloads exceed 10 GiB, or the persistent browser profile exceeds
  4 GiB. Every threshold and retry interval has an explicit loop option; zero
  disables a storage threshold. BARE-METAL neither detaches nor retries unless
  explicitly requested.

  Loop-only controls:
    --turns N                    Successful-turn limit; 0 means until stopped.
    --prompt TEXT                Prompt for each turn; --message is equivalent.
    --prompt-file PATH           Read the prompt from one owned regular file.
    --auto-resume                Retry quota/transient failures (safe default).
    --no-auto-resume             Stop after the first failed attempt.
    --detach                     Run under the user systemd manager.
    --recover-on-boot            Detach and install an opt-in recovery unit.
    --retry-limit N              Maximum retry attempts; 0 disables retries.
    --retry-base-seconds N       Initial exponential-backoff interval.
    --retry-max-seconds N        Per-retry backoff ceiling.
    --retry-max-wait-seconds N   Aggregate retry-wait ceiling; 0 is unlimited.
    --min-free-mib N             Required free space before a turn.
    --max-project-growth-mib N   Growth ceiling relative to loop start.
    --max-downloads-mib N        Persistent browser-download ceiling.
    --max-profile-mib N          Persistent browser-profile ceiling.

  `loop send` also accepts --message-file PATH. `loop status`, `send`, and
  `stop` target the active project loop; `loop recover` starts the last exact
  inactive detached supervisor artifact after validating its digest and owner.

  Every sandbox receives SANDCODEX_LOOPER, which names the protected launcher.
  A model command in an ordinary interactive safe run may choose any finite
  continuation count, or 0 to continue until stopped or a turn fails. It may
  also inject a message at the next existing turn boundary, read advisory
  project-local status, or drain after the current turn:
    "$SANDCODEX_LOOPER" loop --turns 30 --prompt 'Continue with the next risk.'
    "$SANDCODEX_LOOPER" loop --turns 0 --prompt 'Continue until done.'
    "$SANDCODEX_LOOPER" loop send --message 'Run the full regression suite.'
    "$SANDCODEX_LOOPER" loop status
    "$SANDCODEX_LOOPER" loop stop

  Requests are inert JSON, validated by the already-authorized outer launcher.
  They cannot launch concurrently, change posture or provider flags, expose
  credentials, or add turns beyond the outer run's delegated authority. An
  ordinary interactive safe run delegates an unbounded sequential-turn budget;
  an explicit host loop still owns its exact budget and cannot be enlarged by
  an inner request. BARE-METAL loops have no namespace, proxy, or cgroup.

COMPANION CAMPAIGNS
  An interactive command room receives sandcodex-team plus a role-scoped MCP
  server. By default it may run four workers with unbounded Codex invocations
  (turns=0) until completion or explicit stop, without ending the primary
  frontend. sandcodex-worker and SANDCODEX_CAMPAIGN remain compatible subsets:
    sandcodex-team start review --turns 0 \
      --prompt-file plans/review.md --effort high
    sandcodex-team start audit --turns 10 --depends review \
      --prompt 'Audit review and report concrete defects.'
    sandcodex-team list
    sandcodex-team top
    sandcodex-team inspect review
    sandcodex-team wait-any --timeout 1800
    sandcodex-team send review \
      --message 'Prioritize the failing integration test.'
    sandcodex-team inbox
    sandcodex-team stop review
    sandcodex-team collect review \
      --output .sandcodex/work/campaign-artifacts/review.bundle
    sandcodex-team diff review
    sandcodex-team test review -- ./tests/run.sh
    sandcodex-team stage review
    sandcodex-team adopt review --strategy cherry-pick
    sandcodex-team grant status
    sandcodex-team grant capacity --max 2
    # Host terminal only, after reviewing/collecting and stopping the worker:
    sandcodex campaign remove --name review

  The broker snapshots an exact clean Git commit into an independent clone
  outside both the selected project and trusted credential state. The worker
  has its own project lock, branch, Codex session, and safe boundary. A worker
  cannot write the command-room checkout or create another worker. Browser and
  desktop default to none; isolated modes require matching live primary grants
  and receive separate child realms. Collection requires committed work and
  emits a Git bundle plus a broker-owned digest receipt. Only explicit adopt
  mutates a clean primary checkout.
  In campaign.v1, --turns aliases codex_invocations. It does not count ChatGPT
  submissions, campaign episodes, or inner-jail actions.

  Managed-local, remote, and Gemini interactive rooms expose the same control surface.
  The local foreman TUI stays open while its companion runs; both Codex
  processes share the exact parent-owned Ollama runtime. The fixed
  one-generation server policy queues inference instead of loading another
  model, while shell and tool work may overlap. A remote companion instead
  inherits the parent room's exact Responses endpoint snapshot, qualification
  receipt, and copied credential in trusted state. It never receives GPT
  authentication and never reads the operator's shell environment. A fixed
  remote companion cannot retarget provider or model. A remote-auto companion
  uses the copied, digest-checked key bank to choose a fresh model/key when it
  starts or restarts; its retry envelope is deliberately short so provider
  quota/transient failures reach the broker replay path instead of trapping the
  operator behind one exhausted free model. A Gemini companion inherits the
  parent-owned qualified adapter and free-tier-model-only route. Before output from an
  upstream request becomes visible, it keeps the strongest model while automatically
  rotating through the five-account key bank on quota, rate, authentication, or
  permission pressure; after those routes are cooling it can move down the model
  ladder, including with an exact signed tool history.
  Hosted concurrency, quality,
  pricing, and rate limits remain provider-defined.

  Team controls are action-scoped. `start` accepts dependencies, provider
  constraints, effort, token/time budgets, isolated GUI modes, and one prompt
  source. `graph` validates a complete DAG before creating any workers.
  Messages carry IDs, acknowledgements, reply links, and optional retained
  attachments. `top` reports live provider/model, lifecycle, usage, budgets,
  mailbox, and scheduler state. `diff`, `test`, `stage`, and `adopt` implement
  the explicit review boundary. `remove` remains operator-only.

  --companion-campaigns and --companion-max-turns alter launch authority. The
  active model may spend but cannot widen it. `grant` can revoke/restore new
  starts, lower/restore active capacity up to the launch ceiling, suspend
  remote-auto worker selection, and enable/revoke separately authorized
  isolated worker GUI realms without stopping the primary room. Host status,
  collection, and cleanup controls remain available after the primary exits;
  GPT recovery also remains explicit, while local/remote/Gemini
  alternate-provider workers cannot restart after their parent-owned provider
  authority exits. New campaign creation requires a live authorized command
  room. `campaign remove` is operator-only and refuses a
  live worker; it deletes that terminal campaign's staged clone, worker session,
  supervisor record, and broker evidence after confirmation. Without
  --companion-storage-budget, aggregate workspace/cache use is observed but not
  hard-capped. Sealed invariant inputs and recursive campaign principals remain
  explicitly outside campaign.v1; shared/attached browser custody is not
  implied by isolated worker realms.

INNER JAIL
  Every active session receives `sandcodex-jail`, a smaller boundary for
  browser-emitted or otherwise less-trusted actions. The primary Codex agent
  is the delegator. The default grant is deliberately capable: a named
  persistent workspace and paired cache, the rich `build` profile, delegated
  egress, and heavy resources with no Sandcodex action ceilings. The agent may
  reduce tools, network, resources, cwd, CPU, memory, PIDs, or timeout. The
  action supplies argv only and cannot add host paths, environment, secrets,
  destinations, tools, or authority above its immutable grant. Resource fields
  are independent: a numeric action flag changes only that dimension. Finite
  --jail-max-* launch ceilings are inherited unless the action reduces them.

    sandcodex-jail inspect
    sandcodex-jail capability-card
    sandcodex-jail self-test
    sandcodex-jail workspace create review --role ephemeral-verification
    dir=$(sandcodex-jail workspace path review)
    git archive HEAD | tar -x -C "$dir"
    sandcodex-jail run --workspace review --tools python -- \
      python3 -c 'import json; print(json.load(open("artifact.json")))'
    sandcodex-jail workspace usage --json
    sandcodex-jail workspace retire review --receipt
    sandcodex-jail workspace purge review --receipt

  Profiles are minimal, python, node, and build; build includes normal source,
  archive, Git, search, C/C++, Go, and Rust tooling. Every action gets a fresh
  user, mount, PID, network, IPC, and UTS namespace; an exact Nix closure;
  uid/gid 65534 with no capabilities; a cleared environment; broker-created
  cgroup; leaf seccomp; and Landlock allow-only read/write canaries checked
  before argv.
  Delegated HTTP(S)/SOCKS uses only the fixed proxy while direct sockets and UDP
  remain blocked. Every reachable destination can receive staged bytes.

  `sandcodex-env --capabilities` explains the authority split, recipes, and
  current limitations; `sandcodex-jail capability-card --json` is the focused
  orchestration artifact. Recursive calls are brokered through depth five and
  may only narrow the parent grant. `--resources bounded` selects the tested
  30s / 100% CPU / 512 MiB / zero-swap / 256 PID / 128 MiB-per-file /
  32 MiB-tmp / 16 MiB-home preset. `--resources heavy` clears every field only
  when the launch and parent grants permit an unbounded vector.
  Persistent workspace/cache storage is unlimited unless launch selects
  --jail-storage-budget; every workspace in that budget group shares the same
  hard filesystem capacity. Managed Git rejects repository selectors and
  nested repository creation. Use sandcodex-git-standalone --work-tree PATH --
  for an intentional independent repository beneath the selected project.
  BARE-METAL authorizes host-proxied jail egress by default; use
  `--jail-network none` to disable it. A root-capable outer agent can always
  bypass or replace a voluntary inner helper.

AUTH
  A normal first run authenticates automatically before entering the sandbox.
  An existing ~/.codex/auth.json is imported and validated. Otherwise, local
  graphical sessions open browser login while SSH and headless terminals use
  device login. The requested run continues after authentication succeeds.

  Explicit authentication remains available:
    sandcodex login
    sandcodex login --device
    sandcodex logout
    sandcodex reauth
  Import an existing file cache without changing its source:
    sandcodex auth import ~/.codex/auth.json

  Trusted source: ~/.sandstuff/sandcodex/auth-home/auth.json
  Per-project credential copies and sessions stay in the trusted global root,
  never in PROJECT/.sandcodex. Codex's in-session /logout is propagated back
  to the trusted source, and refreshed frontend tokens are promoted atomically.
  `reauth` clears stale copies and performs a fresh host-side login in one step.

NETWORK
  Safe shell commands have unrestricted proxied HTTP, HTTPS CONNECT, and SOCKS
  egress by default, including local/private destinations. Direct TCP/UDP still
  has no route, and host Unix sockets such as Docker, SSH agent, D-Bus, and the
  desktop remain absent. This is an operator-selected disclosure posture:
  project-readable bytes may be sent to any reachable destination.

  --restricted-network reduces shell traffic to provider hosts plus explicit
  --allow-domain entries, rejects private-address resolutions, and withholds the
  Nix daemon unless --nix-daemon is explicit. --jail-network none independently
  disables delegated inner-jail egress. Safe mode never shares the parent
  network namespace in either posture.

BROWSER
  Safe mode prepares a complete Playwright-controlled Chromium browser by
  default. Its human viewer starts hidden; use `sandcodex-browser show` only
  when collaboration requires a visible nested browser and `hide` afterward.
  It has unrestricted web and LAN access, a persistent profile scoped
  to the canonical project, atomic downloads in PROJECT/.sandcodex/work/downloads,
  and access to HTTP/WebSocket servers started inside the agent namespace.
  Chromium runs in a second bubblewrap boundary: project source is read-only,
  trusted Sandcodex auth/session state is absent, and only browser home,
  profile, output, and download locations are writable. A private browser
  network namespace reaches the broker through one Unix socket; host loopback
  is absent, while agent-loopback web servers are routed deliberately.

  Codex can install or reload unpacked extensions directly through CDP. A
  single *-sandcodex-browser.zip in the project is validated and connected
  automatically.
  Native messaging and the shell share
  PROJECT/.sandcodex/work/browser-home/.local/share/glasstty. Use --no-browser
  to omit Chromium and Playwright from the Nix closure. In BARE-METAL mode this
  browser is deliberately unsandboxed, like the rest of Codex.
  If an identity-matching project extension is built after launch, activate it
  and its isolated native host without ending the command room:
    sandcodex-browser activate /absolute/project/path/extension
  Inspect the sole persistent MCP/Chromium owner without exposing page content:
    sandcodex-browser status --json
    sandcodex-browser viewer-status --json
    sandcodex-browser show
    sandcodex-browser hide
  After terminal owner death, explicitly recover from the last seen generation:
    sandcodex-browser recover --expected-generation N --json
  Activation proves one native round trip, preserves every existing tab route,
  and returns an exact receipt. Client disconnects do not close Chromium.

ISOLATED DESKTOP
  Native Linux GUI applications run in a separate credentialless Wayland workstation
  with its own Bubblewrap mount, PID, IPC, UTS, user, and network namespaces.
  Codex observes and operates that workstation through a required local MCP
  server providing screenshots, windows, pointer, clicks, drag, scroll, typing,
  clipboard, focus, move/resize/close, and explicit process lifecycle tools.

  The workstation can read and write ordinary selected-project content, but the
  real host display, host D-Bus, SSH agent, Docker socket, browser profile,
  frontend credentials, and .sandcodex internals are absent. Each application
  receives a restricted per-launch Wayland socket;
  explicit wayland+x11 launches get their own authenticated Xwayland satellite.
  The native human viewer starts hidden and is read-only:
    sandcodex-desktop status --json
    sandcodex-desktop show
    sandcodex-desktop hide
  Use --headful to open a separate Xephyr workstation where human and agent
  input remain live concurrently. It requires safe mode and local X/Xwayland;
  the browser window remains independently hidden until requested.
  Use doctor --desktop-only for the focused behavioral proof, and --no-desktop
  to omit the GUI closure and MCP server.

NIX
  Requires x86-64 or ARM64 Linux, Nix with flakes, working unprivileged user
  namespaces, and a running systemd user manager on cgroup v2 for safe-mode
  resource isolation.
  The script pins Nixpkgs, Codex, Playwright MCP, Node, Python, bubblewrap,
  landrun, Squid, and socat; it never writes a host Nix profile. Development
  safe mode exposes only the Nix daemon socket, and only when the daemon
  authoritatively
  reports this user as untrusted and host policy requires sandboxed, local,
  unhooked builds with store-only extra paths. This enables normal flake
  evaluation/build/develop workflows without exposing host profiles or
  configuration.

  Nix daemon access is an explicit host broker, not ordinary sandbox egress.
  Fixed-output fetches and builders run under daemon policy and necessarily add
  immutable closures and daemon metadata under /nix. Their CPU, memory, disk,
  and network traffic, including localhost/LAN reachability, are host-daemon
  work outside Sandcodex's cgroup, network namespace, and egress proxy.
  --restricted-network withholds this broker unless --nix-daemon is also given;
  --no-nix-daemon withholds it in any safe mode.

  `nix profile add` writes an isolated profile under
  PROJECT/.sandcodex/work/state/nix/profile; its bin directory is already on
  model-command PATH. In a real Git repository, creating or updating flake.lock
  works normally because project Git metadata is writable by default.
  A protected local registry pins `nixpkgs` to the launcher revision, so
  `nix shell nixpkgs#ripgrep`, `nix build`, and `nix develop` work without
  channel lookup or project-root synthetic files.

  Discovery inside the sandbox:
    sandcodex-env --briefing      short action-first first-contact card
    sandcodex-env --capabilities  direct operational recipes and limits
    sandcodex-env --summary       compact one-glance map
    sandcodex-env --json          exact machine-readable capability contract

  Extra pinned tools:
    SANDCODEX_NIX_PACKAGES="python313 go cargo rustc gcc gnumake" sandcodex

STATE AND LOGS
  Trusted:   ~/.sandstuff/sandcodex/
  Disposable: PROJECT/.sandcodex/ (worker state and protected launcher copy)
  Inner jail staging: PROJECT/.sandcodex/work/inner-jail/workspaces/
  Inner jail caches:  PROJECT/.sandcodex/work/inner-jail/caches/
  Companion evidence: ~/.sandstuff/sandcodex/campaigns/
  Companion clones:   ~/.sandstuff/sandcodex-workspaces/
  Finite companion group:
    ~/.sandstuff/sandcodex/campaign-storage/PROJECT_KEY/GROUP/
  Override trusted state with SANDCODEX_GLOBAL_ROOT=/absolute/path.
  Launcher-only logs never copy provider output, prompts, or credentials.
  Read this project's latest log without starting Nix or creating another log:
    sandcodex logs
    sandcodex logs follow
    sandcodex logs list
  Safe runs cap Codex prompt history at 32 MiB; session rollouts are retained
  until explicitly cleaned. `sandcodex doctor` reports state and cache usage.
  `sandcodex state export FILE.zip` creates a private, checksummed portable
  archive containing only this project's Codex sessions and persistent scratch.
  Authentication, browser profiles/downloads, logs, caches, Git metadata, Nix
  profiles, generated configuration, and runtime state are never candidates.
  Import validates the complete archive before mutation and relocates structured
  session paths to the selected project. Use `state migrate --dry-run` before an
  additive state-format migration; every committed migration has a trusted
  transaction journal and can be reverted with `state rollback`.
  Ordinary `sandcodex clean` removes disposable browser output/runtime state,
  project/frontend caches, and inner-jail caches while retaining named jail
  workspaces, downloads, browser login state, GlassTTY state, and Codex sessions.
  Use --downloads, --browser, --desktop, --sessions, or --logs for those exact
  durable scopes. --everything removes every current-project
  durable scope, including supervisor recovery records and state-transaction
  journals, plus every stopped or terminal companion clone and its broker
  evidence. It refuses cleanup if any companion remains active or fails its
  identity preflight. Collected campaign artifacts remain in the project.
  Shared authentication, the cleanup audit log, and its empty operation-lock
  sentinel are retained. Persistent boot units are disabled before their
  trusted records are removed. BARE-METAL does not impose the history cap.

INSTALL
  Install or atomically upgrade this exact standalone artifact:
    sandcodex install
  The default destination is ~/.local/bin/sandcodex. No Nix environment or
  Sandcodex state is created. --bin-dir selects an existing absolute directory.

BARE METAL
  --BARE-METAL runs Codex without a sandbox and invokes `sudo -v` by default.
  Sudo reads the password directly; Sandcodex never sees or logs it. Before
  Codex starts, Sandcodex verifies the cached ticket can run a non-interactive
  command as uid 0; Codex is instructed to use `sudo -n -- COMMAND` for root
  work. The reusable ticket is refreshed only while Codex runs. Add
  `--no-sudo-ticket` when the host-uncontained room should not touch sudo at
  launch. The invoking host PATH and environment remain available. Outer
  namespace, seccomp, proxy, and resource limits do not apply. `sandcodex-jail`
  remains available for deliberately delegated actions, with host-proxied
  egress unless `--jail-network none` was selected.

LIMITS
  Safe mode is not a VM. The selected project can consume its filesystem;
  Sandcodex does not create a quota for that outer tree. Optional jail and
  companion storage budgets use separate fixed-capacity ext4 images. Native
  code still shares the host kernel. Safe mode requires .git metadata to be a
  directory inside the chosen project; use the containing repository or bare
  metal for a linked worktree. Use a VM for hostile binaries or whole-project
  storage isolation.
EOF
}

sc_artifact_sha256() {
  sha256sum -- "$SC_SCRIPT" | awk '{print $1}'
}

